From d76facc6e90df6f7a1e5436c9594c6f2eb115663 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:55:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] fix(cli): wait for the latest generation now that updates
 redeploy

The controller now redeploys a service whose spec changes: a Ready or
Degraded service goes through Updating, a Failed one goes back to
Pending, and a Degraded one is redeployed after a backoff. Every
status write records the generation read at the start of its round,
so only phase Ready, a True Ready condition and
metadata.observedGeneration equal to metadata.generation in the same
response mean that the current spec is deployed.

wait --for ready now requires exactly that. It compares with == and
reads observedGeneration from metadata only, so a response that does
not report both generations, or reports an observed generation ahead
of the spec, no longer counts as ready. It keeps waiting through a
stale Ready, Updating, Degraded and a Failed service whose spec has
changed since, and still fails at once on Terminating and on Failed
at the current generation; that error now adds that the controller
deploys the service again only when its spec changes.

An update denied by the policy, held for approval or lacking a
scheduler or launcher writes no status: the service stays Ready (or
Degraded) with the old observedGeneration and the reason is only in
the control plane log. The timeout message for that state now says
that the update has not started and where to find the reason.

The README no longer claims that the controller does not redeploy a
Ready service; the wait paragraph, the exit code table, --help, the
CHANGELOG entry and module-mapping describe the rule above. The e2e
test applies a changed spec after the first wait and checks that the
second wait returns at generation and observedGeneration 2 with a new
instance.
